 all; feedback loops generate new training data. A research agenda is in Section 5, Table 4. Still to read: Section 1.2 Literature Selection (p.5) for search methods, Section 2 (reasoning, p.8-19), Section 3 (acting, p.20-30), Section 4 (interacting, p.31-47), Section 5 (p.48-52).\",\n    evidence_ids=[\"ev-03c9580cc4ea3469a3d3\", \"ev-84af1a450301aa5252ba\", \"ev-c3c928f38ebc0ac0c6cd\"],\n    section=\"overview\"\n)\nprint(r)\n```", "tool_calls": null, "raw": {"_type": "ChatCompletion", "id": "d8f368cb-3403-40c0-81b5-ab80575826a5", "choices": [{"_type": "Choice", "finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "logprobs": null, "message": {"_type": "ChatCompletionMessage", "content": "Pages 1–3 delivered. I'll record an initial overview note while the remaining batches queue forward.\n\n```python\nr = write_note(\n    title=\"overview notes\",\n    content=\"Pages 1-3: Paper defines agentic LLMs as LLMs that (1) reason, (2) act, (3) interact; literature is organized according to these three categories. Contributions list adds details: category 1 = reasoning, reflection, retrieval to improve decision making; category 2 = tools and robot integration producing assistants in medicine/finance; category 3 = interaction, multi-agent simulations, role-playing, open-ended agent societies, emergent behavior. Figure 1 gives a virtuous cycle: retrieval enables tool use, reflection improves multi-agent collaboration, reasoning benefits all; feedback loops generate new training data. A research agenda is in Section 5, Table 4. Still to read: Section 1.2 Literature Selection (p.5) for search methods, Section 2 (reasoning, p.8-19), Section 3 (acting, p.20-30), Section 4 (interacting, p.31-47), Section 5 (p.48-52).\",\n    evidence_ids=[\"ev-03c9580cc4ea3469a3d3\", \"ev-84af1a450301aa5252ba\", \"ev-c3c928f38ebc0ac0c6cd\"],\n    section=\"overview\"\n)\nprint(r)\n```", "refusal": null, "role": "assistant", "annotations": null, "audio": null, "function_call": null, "tool_calls": null}}], "creat