3: Paper defines agentic LLMs as LLMs that (1) reason, (2) act, (3) interact; literature is organized according to these three categories. Contributions list adds details: category 1 = reasoning, reflection, retrieval to improve decision making; category 2 = tools and robot integration producing assistants in medicine/finance; category 3 = interaction, multi-agent simulations, role-playing, open-ended agent societies, emergent behavior. Figure 1 gives a virtuous cycle: retrieval enables tool use, reflection improves multi-agent collaboration, reasoning benefits all; feedback loops generate new training data. A research agenda is in Section 5, Table 4. Still to read: Section 1.2 Literature Selection (p.5) for search methods, Section 2 (reasoning, p.8-19), Section 3 (acting, p.20-30), Section 4 (interacting, p.31-47), Section 5 (p.48-52).\",\n    evidence_ids=[\"ev-03c9580cc4ea3469a3d3\", \"ev-84af1a450301aa5252ba\", \"ev-c3c928f38ebc0ac0c6cd\"],\n    section=\"overview\"\n)\nprint(r)", "observations": "Execution logs:\n{'note_id': 'overview', 'sources': ['paper.pdf'], 'chars': 856}\nLast output from code snippet:\nNone", "observations_images": null, "action_output": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 7857, "output_tokens": 610, "total_tokens": 8467}, "is_final_answer": false}}
