ill contain pages, but output shown to model as evidence, not Python. We can store in same action: r = read_pdf(17,20); then PDF.update...; print only keys. That's ideal—one action gets pages and stores, without printing. But we need text to reason later. We can query PDF with keyword searches in later actions.\n\nWe must produce Python block now: call read_pdf for 17-20, initialize PDF, store previous r if it has pages 14-16, store new pages, print keys. Does calling read_pdf inside code work? Yes available tool. Need maybe PDF global persists. Let's do:\n\nPDF = {}\ntry:\n  for p in r['pages']: PDF[p['page']]=p['text']\nexcept Exception: pass\nr2 = read_pdf(start_page=17,end_page=20)\nfor p in r2['pages']: PDF[p['page']]=p['text']\nprint(sorted(PDF))\n\nException handling allowed. But \"dir\" was forbidden; try/except fine. Note r may be dict. Let's do."}, "logprobs": null, "finish_reason": "stop"}], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 7657, "completion_tokens": 642, "total_tokens": 8299, "prompt_tokens_details": {"cached_tokens": 4480}, "completion_tokens_details": {"reasoning_tokens": 541}, "prompt_cache_hit_tokens": 4480, "prompt_cache_miss_tokens": 3177}, "system_fingerprint": "bc19ba97cbe016479903222bb1578db6"}, "classification": "ok"}
